72. CACHE LOCALITY
------------------------------------------------------------

Arrays are contiguous in memory.

Therefore sequential traversal often has excellent cache
locality.

Example:

for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
{
    sum += arr[i];
}

This is generally cache-friendly.

Cache behavior is an important real-world performance concept.


------------------------------------------------------------
73. ROW-MAJOR CACHE ACCESS
------------------------------------------------------------

For:

int matrix[1000][1000];


Usually cache-friendly:

for (size_t i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
{
    for (size_t j = 0; j < 1000; j++)
    {
        process(matrix[i][j]);
    }
}


This follows row-major storage order.

Column-first traversal may have worse cache locality.


------------------------------------------------------------
74. ARRAY VS LINKED LIST
------------------------------------------------------------

Array:

    - contiguous memory
    - O(1) random access
    - good cache locality
    - insertion/deletion can be expensive

Linked list:

    - nodes need not be contiguous
    - sequential access
    - insertion/deletion can be efficient when node position
      is already known
    - extra pointer storage
    - weaker cache locality


Choose according to the workload.


