------------------------------------------------------------

At block scope:

void f(void)
{
    static int arr[100];
}


The array has static storage duration.

It exists for the entire execution of the program, but its
name has block scope.


At file scope:

static int arr[100];

This additionally gives internal linkage to the file-scope
identifier.


------------------------------------------------------------
76. GLOBAL ARRAY
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

int data[100];

This is a file-scope array with static storage duration.

Global mutable arrays should be used carefully because they
create shared state.


------------------------------------------------------------
77. AUTOMATIC ARRAY
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

void f(void)
{
    int arr[100];
}


This normally has automatic storage duration.

Its lifetime ends when execution leaves the block.

Do not return a pointer to such an array.

Wrong:

int *f(void)
{
    int arr[10];

    return arr;
}


------------------------------------------------------------
78. DYNAMIC ARRAY
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void)
{
    size_t n = 5;

    int *arr = malloc(n * sizeof *arr);

    if (arr == NULL)
        return 1;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        arr[i] = (int)i;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);

    free(arr);

    return 0;
}


------------------------------------------------------------
79. MEMORY LEAK
------------------------------------------------------------

If dynamically allocated memory is not released:

int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof *p);

/* forgot free(p); */


Memory becomes unreachable.

This is called a memory leak.

Correct:

free(p);


------------------------------------------------------------
80. DOUBLE FREE
------------------------------------------------------------

Wrong:

int *p = malloc(10 * sizeof *p);

free(p);
free(p);


Calling free twice on the same allocation causes undefined
behavior.


A common defensive pattern is:

free(p);
p = NULL;


Then:

free(p);

is defined because free(NULL) does nothing.

However, setting one pointer to NULL does not invalidate other
aliases to the same allocation.


------------------------------------------------------------
81. NULL POINTER VS EMPTY ARRAY
------------------------------------------------------------

These are different concepts.

NULL pointer:

int *p = NULL;


It points to no object.

An empty logical array might mean:

n == 0

A function should clearly define whether it accepts:

    arr == NULL with n == 0

or requires a valid pointer regardless of n.


------------------------------------------------------------
82. ARRAY INPUT
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

int arr[5];

for (size_t i = 0; i < 5; i++)
{
    scanf("%d", &arr[i]);
}


Input:

10 20 30 40 50


Array:

10 20 30 40 50


Always consider checking scanf's return value in robust code.


------------------------------------------------------------
83. FIND SECOND LARGEST ELEMENT
------------------------------------------------------------

This problem requires careful handling of:

    - duplicate values
    - negative values
    - arrays with fewer than two elements

Do not simply assume:

second = arr[1]


Interviewers often test edge cases.


------------------------------------------------------------
84. COMMON ARRAY BUGS
------------------------------------------------------------

BUG 1:

for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++)

Wrong if array has n elements.

Correct:

i < n


BUG 2:

arr[n]

Out of bounds for an n-element array.


BUG 3:

Using sizeof inside an array parameter.

sizeof(arr)

does not give the original array size.


BUG 4:

Returning local array address.

int *f(void)
{
    int a[10];
    return a;
}


BUG 5:

Forgetting string null terminator.


BUG 6:

Writing beyond allocated memory.


BUG 7:

Using an uninitialized automatic array.


BUG 8:

Using a freed dynamic array.


BUG 9:

Double free.


BUG 10:

Integer overflow during allocation-size calculation.


------------------------------------------------------------
85. UNINITIALIZED AUTOMATIC ARRAY
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

int arr[5];

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
{
    printf("%d ", arr[i]);
}


The elements have indeterminate values.

Reading an indeterminate value can lead to undefined behavior
in certain circumstances.

Initialize before reading:

int arr[5] = {0};


------------------------------------------------------------
86. ARRAY OF POINTERS VS POINTER TO ARRAY
------------------------------------------------------------

Array of pointers:

int *p[5];

Meaning:

    p is an array of 5 pointers to int.


Pointer to array:

int (*p)[5];

Meaning:

    p is a pointer to an array of 5 int.


The parentheses are critical.


------------------------------------------------------------
87. ARRAY PARAMETER WITH STATIC
------------------------------------------------------------

Advanced C syntax:

void process(int arr[static 10])
{
    ...
}


This communicates a contract to the compiler:

when calling this function, the argument should provide access
to at least 10 elements.

This is different from merely writing:

int arr[10]

in a function parameter, because ordinary array parameters
are adjusted to pointers.


------------------------------------------------------------
88. RESTRICT WITH ARRAYS
------------------------------------------------------------

Advanced C:

void copy_data(size_t n,
               int *restrict dst,
               const int *restrict src)
{
    ...
}


`restrict` is used to express assumptions about pointer
aliasing and can enable optimizations when the requirements
are actually satisfied.

Incorrect use of restrict can lead to undefined behavior.

Learn it deeply later with pointers and memory.


------------------------------------------------------------
89. ARRAY COPYING WITH LOOP
------------------------------------------------------------

void copyArray(int dest[],
               const int src[],
               size_t n)
{
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        dest[i] = src[i];
    }
}


This requires:

    dest

to point to enough writable storage.


------------------------------------------------------------
90. ARRAY ROTATION
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

Original:

1 2 3 4 5

Left rotate by one:

2 3 4 5 1

Right rotate by one:

5 1 2 3 4


This is a common coding interview problem.

Possible techniques:

    - temporary array
    - repeated shifting
    - reversal algorithm


------------------------------------------------------------
91. TWO-SUM PROBLEM
------------------------------------------------------------

Given:

2 7 11 15

Target:

9

Answer:

2 + 7 = 9


Simple brute-force:

O(n²)


Using a suitable hash-table/data-structure approach:

approximately O(n) average time in typical implementations.


This is a common interview pattern.


------------------------------------------------------------
92. DUPLICATE ELEMENTS
------------------------------------------------------------

Given:

1 2 3 2 4 1

Duplicates:

1 and 2


Possible approaches depend on constraints:

    - nested loops
    - sorting
    - frequency array
    - hash table


Choosing the approach based on constraints is an important
job-ready skill.


------------------------------------------------------------
93. PREFIX SUM
------------------------------------------------------------

Given:

arr:

1 2 3 4 5

Prefix sum:

1 3 6 10 15


Formula:

prefix[i] = prefix[i - 1] + arr[i]


Useful for answering range-sum queries efficiently.


------------------------------------------------------------
94. ARRAY AS FUNCTION RETURN ALTERNATIVE
------------------------------------------------------------

A function cannot directly return an array type.

Instead:

A. Caller provides output array:

void generate(int out[], size_t n);


B. Return a pointer to dynamically allocated memory:

int *generate(size_t n);


C. Return a structure containing an array when appropriate.

D. Use static storage only when shared lifetime/state is
   intentionally desired.


------------------------------------------------------------
95. PRACTICAL PROGRAM — ARRAY STATISTICS
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

    size_t n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    int min = arr[0];
    int max = arr[0];
    long long sum = 0;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (arr[i] < min)
            min = arr[i];

        if (arr[i] > max)
            max = arr[i];

        sum += arr[i];
    }

    printf("Min = %d\n", min);
    printf("Max = %d\n", max);
    printf("Sum = %lld\n", sum);
    printf("Average = %.2f\n", (double)sum / n);

    return 0;
}


------------------------------------------------------------
96. PRACTICAL PROGRAM — REVERSE ARRAY
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>

void reverse(int arr[], size_t n)
{
    size_t i = 0;
    size_t j = n;

    if (n == 0)
        return;

    j--;

    while (i < j)
    {
        int temp = arr[i];

        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;

        i++;
        j--;
    }
}

int main(void)
{
    int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    size_t n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    reverse(arr, n);

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);

    return 0;
}

Output:

5 4 3 2 1


------------------------------------------------------------
97. PRACTICAL PROGRAM — LINEAR SEARCH
------------------------------------------------------------

#include <stdio.h>

int search(const int arr[], size_t n, int key)
{
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (arr[i] == key)
            return (int)i;
    }

    return -1;
}

int main(void)
{
    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

    size_t n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    int index = search(arr, n, 30);

    printf("Index = %d\n", index);

    return 0;
}

Output:

Index = 2


